.TH KISA-CCE-SCAN 8 "2026-09-03" "KISA CCE Linux Scanner" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
kisa-cce-scan \- assess KISA CCE 2026 Unix-server criteria on supported
Linux systems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kisa-cce-scan
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kisa-cce-scan
collects persistent configuration, manager-normalized configuration, and
runtime state for the 67 KISA CCE 2026 Unix-server criteria.
It supports the explicitly listed Debian, Ubuntu, Enterprise Linux, and
derivative releases described below.
.PP
The installed support list describes accepted platform identities and
implemented configuration families.  It is not vendor certification or proof
that every listed release has completed booted-host acceptance testing.
.PP
The scanner does not remediate configuration, reload services, refresh package
metadata, or run an explicit network-fetch operation.
A live U-15 scan uses host NSS and may therefore consult an external identity
backend.
A normal scan writes protected Markdown and JSONL reports.
.PP
One invocation uses one immutable scan epoch.  Configuration parser results,
systemd facts, and listener facts are reused only inside that process.  A new
invocation collects runtime state again; no cache persists across runs.
.SH SUPPORTED PLATFORMS
The lifecycle snapshot dated 2026-09-03 accepts Debian 12 and 13; Ubuntu
22.04, 24.04, and 26.04 LTS; and the current base-stream RHEL releases 8.10,
9.8, and 10.2.
.PP
The Enterprise Linux derivative list contains the current AlmaLinux, Rocky
Linux, and Oracle Linux 8.10, 9.8, and 10.2 releases, plus CentOS Stream 9 and
10.
The Ubuntu derivative list contains Linux Mint 21, 21.1, 21.2, 21.3, 22,
22.1, 22.2, and 22.3; Pop!_OS 22.04 and 24.04; Zorin OS 17 and 18;
elementary OS 7, 7.1, and 8; and KDE neon User Edition 24.04.
.PP
Approved Ubuntu derivatives must identify the expected Ubuntu base through
.B UBUNTU_CODENAME
and list both Ubuntu and Debian in
.BR ID_LIKE .
An arbitrary
.B ID_LIKE
value never authorizes an unlisted product.
.PP
The accepted Ubuntu derivative base mappings are: Linux Mint 21.x, Pop!_OS
22.04, Zorin OS 17, and elementary OS 7.x use
.BR jammy ;
Linux Mint 22.x, Pop!_OS 24.04, Zorin OS 18, elementary OS 8, and KDE neon User
Edition 24.04 use
.BR noble .
.PP
Subscription-only extended support, third-party extended maintenance,
development releases, point releases retired by their projects when
superseded, and non-User KDE neon editions are not in the default support
matrix.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-root " PATH"
Inspect an existing offline filesystem directory rooted at the absolute
.IR PATH .
Runtime collection is disabled automatically.
The special path
.B /
remains a live-root scan.
.TP
.BI \-\-output-dir " PATH"
Write reports below the absolute
.IR PATH .
For a normal scan, the absolute path must contain no symbolic-link component.
The directory must belong to the invoking user, grant owner read, write, and
search access, and grant no permissions to group or other users.
Existing ancestors must not be replaceable through an untrusted group- or
other-writable path; trusted sticky temporary directories remain valid.
A missing directory is created with mode 0700.
The scanner pins the validated directory by file descriptor and refuses to
print report paths if the lexical binding changes before finalization.
In sysctl explanation mode only the absolute-path syntax is validated, and the
scanner does not create or write the directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-checks " U-01,U-02"
Run only the comma-separated criterion codes.
Codes are case-insensitive, whitespace is removed, and duplicates are ignored.
Results retain catalog order rather than argument order.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " audit|complete"
Select the result contract.
Audit mode preserves indeterminate results as
.BR MANUAL .
Complete mode requires all 67 criteria, a policy directory, live runtime
collection or a matching evidence bundle, and emits no
.B MANUAL
result.
.TP
.BI \-\-policy-dir " PATH"
Load strict TSV attestations and supported typed facts from the absolute
directory
.IR PATH .
Complete mode requires this option.
.TP
.BI \-\-evidence-bundle " PATH"
Use the validated owner-only runtime evidence directory at
.I PATH
with an offline root.
The bundle must match the image machine ID and os-release.
.TP
.BI \-\-evidence-max-age " SECONDS"
Reject a runtime evidence bundle older than
.IR SECONDS .
The default is 3600 and the maximum is 604800.
.TP
.B \-\-no-runtime
Disable live service, listener, kernel-value, and native-validator collection.
For a live-root scan, local mount topology is still collected to define
complete filesystem traversal boundaries.
A scan of the live root still requires root privileges.
.TP
.BI \-\-explain-sysctl " KEY"
Print the effective persistent and runtime interpretation of one sysctl key
instead of producing CCE reports.
This option cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-checks .
.TP
.B \-\-allow-unsupported
Continue after warning that the detected platform is unsupported.
This bypass does not make results authoritative for that platform.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Write platform context, each check code, status, and catalog title, and final
counters to standard error.
Verbose output does not include result summaries or evidence.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Enable verbose progress and write structured internal lifecycle, resolver,
cache, collection, and report-validation events to standard error.
Debug mode does not enable shell execution tracing, retain temporary files,
create a separate debug file, or change reports, result states, or exit status.
It does not print result summaries, evidence, assessed configuration content,
or native-command output.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print command help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the scanner version and exit.
.SH TERMINAL OUTPUT
Every line written by the command uses a dmesg-style prefix with six
fractional uptime digits:
.PP
.EX
[    12.345678] kisa-cce-scan: payload
.EE
.PP
This contract applies to help, version, errors, warnings, verbose and debug
progress, sysctl explanations, and report paths. Automation keys and values
remain in the payload. Report paths use standard output; warnings, verbose
progress, and debug events use standard error. The scanner uses a safe
process-time or zero fallback when
.I /proc/uptime
is unavailable.
.PP
Debug records use
.B "DEBUG: schema=1 event=NAME key=value"
payloads. Dynamic field values are percent-encoded outside a restricted safe
byte set and bounded in length. Debug output is sensitive assessment data even
though it excludes raw evidence. Before redirecting standard error, set an
owner-only umask and protect the resulting file like a report. The
.B kisa-cce-collect
command does not implement
.BR \-\-debug .
.PP
Options that take values accept both
.B "\-\-option VALUE"
and
.B "\-\-option=VALUE"
forms.
Empty option values are rejected.
Positional arguments are rejected.
.SH MODES
.SS Complete scan
Complete mode runs U-01 through U-67 and preserves conclusive
.BR GOOD ,
.BR VULNERABLE ,
.BR NOT_APPLICABLE ,
and
.B ERROR
results.
Each technical
.B MANUAL
result receives a SHA-256 review ID.
A matching, unexpired policy attestation converts it to the approved final
decision; a missing, stale, or mismatched attestation becomes
.BR ERROR .
.PP
A live complete scan requires root and runtime collection.
An offline complete scan requires both
.B \-\-policy-dir
and
.BR \-\-evidence-bundle .
.SS Live scan
Scanning
.B /
requires effective UID 0, including when
.B \-\-no-runtime
is supplied.
Runtime collection uses trusted native utilities from fixed system paths when
available.
.SS Offline scan
An absolute
.B \-\-root
other than
.B /
maps logical system paths below that root and disables runtime collection.
Root privileges are not required, but the invoking user needs read and
directory-search permission for the image.
.PP
Offline results alone cannot establish active services, listeners, loaded kernel
values, or other live state. A matching evidence bundle supplies the supported
runtime snapshot without enabling live commands on the analysis host.
Affected criteria may return
.BR MANUAL ,
.BR NOT_APPLICABLE ,
or
.BR ERROR .
.SS Sysctl explanation
The prefixed diagnostic payloads distinguish the filesystem configuration
model, active loader model where available, runtime value, and detected drift.
It follows standard
.I sysctl.d
directory precedence, equal-basename replacement, lexical ordering, masks,
glob assignments, and exclusions.
It does not change kernel parameters or create CCE report files.
The public launcher creates the temporary diagnostic workspace below
.IR /tmp .
.SH REPORTS
When
.B \-\-output-dir
is omitted, a root invocation uses
.IR /var/log/kisa-cce-scanner .
A non-root offline invocation uses
.IR /tmp/kisa-cce-scanner-UID .
.PP
The scanner creates an owner-only temporary workspace and two randomized
mode-0600 files:
.TP
.I kisa-cce-HOST-TIMESTAMP.RANDOM.md
Human-readable scanner metadata, criterion sections, optional evidence, and
summary counts.
.TP
.I kisa-cce-HOST-TIMESTAMP.jsonl.RANDOM
One JSON object per selected criterion followed by one summary object.
.PP
The hostname identifies the machine running the scanner, including during
offline analysis.
The command prints prefixed payloads containing
.B markdown_report=
and
.B jsonl_report=
paths only after final integrity checks succeed.
A signal can arrive while or after these two path lines are printed.
Any printed path names a report that passed final integrity checks, while an
earlier interruption may leave incomplete files whose paths were not printed.
.PP
The JSONL file does not repeat the scanner, platform, root, runtime-mode, or
timestamp header from the Markdown report.
Retain both files when those provenance fields are required.
.PP
Evidence separators are expanded, tab and carriage-return bytes are rendered as
visible escapes in Markdown evidence, remaining unsafe control bytes are
removed or replaced, and UTF-8 is normalized when
.B iconv
is available, and targeted credential redaction is applied before an 8192-byte
limit.
Markdown titles and summaries are escaped, and evidence lines are indented by
four spaces so assessed content remains an inert code block.
JSONL additionally removes an incomplete UTF-8 suffix created by byte-boundary
truncation.
The Markdown evidence section is omitted when the value is empty; JSONL retains
an empty
.B evidence
string.
.SH RESULT STATES
.TP
.B GOOD
Collected evidence conclusively satisfies the implemented criterion.
.TP
.B VULNERABLE
Collected evidence conclusively violates the implemented criterion.
.TP
.B MANUAL
Policy, intent, an approved exception, external state, or unavailable context
requires review.
.TP
.B NOT_APPLICABLE
Absence of the relevant service or feature was established.
.TP
.B ERROR
Required evidence could not be collected or parsed reliably.
.PP
A
.B GOOD
result is not a certification of the entire host.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The invocation completed without a process-level failure.
For a normal scan, no
.B VULNERABLE
or
.B ERROR
result was recorded.
Successful sysctl explanation also returns 0.
.TP
.B 1
A normal scan completed without a process-level failure and recorded at least
one
.B VULNERABLE
result and no
.B ERROR
result.
.TP
.B 2
Invocation, platform detection, sysctl diagnosis, collection, report writing,
report integrity, or at least one criterion produced an error.
.TP
.B 130
The process received SIGINT.
.TP
.B 143
The process received SIGTERM.
.PP
.B ERROR
takes precedence over
.BR VULNERABLE .
.B MANUAL
and
.B NOT_APPLICABLE
do not change the exit status by themselves.
.SH SECURITY
Run the scanner only from a trusted, root-owned installation or a protected
source checkout.
The public launcher clears the caller environment before loading private Bash
code.
Live native commands are restricted to fixed system paths and are subject to
ownership and write-permission checks.
.PP
Reports can contain sensitive host configuration.
Targeted redaction is defense in depth and does not replace review before
storage or transfer.
.PP
Debug output can expose the selected root, platform, criterion activity,
subsystem availability, cache behavior, and failure state. Review it before
storage or transfer. The scanner does not create or set permissions on a file
used for shell-level standard-error redirection.
.PP
When
.B \-\-output-dir
points below an offline root, report files are written into that image.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LANG
Selects the report catalog. Values beginning with
.B en
select English report titles, summaries, and labels. Korean reports are the
default for unset, C, POSIX, Korean, and other locale values. Command help,
progress, warnings, and errors remain English.
.SH FILES
The following paths describe a package installed with
.BR prefix=/usr .
.TP
.I /usr/bin/kisa-cce-scan
Installed public command.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/kisa-cce-linux-scanner/
Default private Bash implementation directory.
.TP
.I /usr/share/kisa-cce-linux-scanner/criteria.tsv
Ordered criterion catalog.
.TP
.I /usr/share/kisa-cce-linux-scanner/VERSION
Scanner version.
.TP
.I /usr/share/kisa-cce-linux-scanner/locale/
Package-specific Korean and English PO catalogs used for report output.
.TP
.I /etc/os-release
Platform identification inside the live or offline root.
.TP
.I /var/log/kisa-cce-scanner/
Default live-root report directory.
.TP
.I /etc/kisa-cce-scanner/policy.d/
Recommended directory for reviewed criterion attestations.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a live audit of all 67 criteria:
.PP
.EX
sudo kisa-cce-scan
.EE
.PP
Run selected criteria:
.PP
.EX
sudo kisa-cce-scan --checks U-01,U-02,U-65
.EE
.PP
Inspect an offline filesystem:
.PP
.EX
kisa-cce-scan --root /srv/images/ubuntu-26.04 --no-runtime
.EE
.PP
Explain one sysctl key:
.PP
.EX
sudo kisa-cce-scan --explain-sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward
.EE
.PP
Capture protected debug diagnostics:
.PP
.EX
umask 077
kisa-cce-scan --debug 2>./kisa-cce-debug.log
.EE
